1
00:00:08,922 --> 00:00:14,389
All right, so
the final topic we're going to cover

2
00:00:14,389 --> 00:00:19,861
in the link layer module
is local area networks.

3
00:00:19,861 --> 00:00:25,051
So as a reminder,
what this is about is being able to

4
00:00:25,051 --> 00:00:31,586
extend beyond the physical limits
of the transmission medium.

5
00:00:31,586 --> 00:00:37,249
So here we're going to talk
about ethernet and wireless.

6
00:00:37,249 --> 00:00:42,630
So in Ethernet, we're going to
introduce the concept of switching.

7
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:46,433
And in wireless, we're going to introduce
the concept of an access point.

8
00:00:46,433 --> 00:00:51,094
And these are the kind of main
devices that we'll talk about that

9
00:00:51,094 --> 00:00:55,250
allow you to basically build
these local area networks.

10
00:00:55,250 --> 00:01:00,053
So why do we need Ethernet switching?

11
00:01:00,053 --> 00:01:04,679
So on the left is the picture
we've shown before

12
00:01:04,679 --> 00:01:09,768
showing Ethernet can be this
shared medium of a bunch

13
00:01:09,768 --> 00:01:15,105
of different nodes attaching
into some shared cable.

14
00:01:15,105 --> 00:01:18,164
So that has scalability challenges.

15
00:01:18,164 --> 00:01:22,791
The more nodes you put on that,
the more chances of collision,

16
00:01:22,791 --> 00:01:27,875
the length it is going to be a challenge,
and all those kinds of things.

17
00:01:27,875 --> 00:01:35,939
So instead what we have more in
practice are the picture on the right.

18
00:01:35,939 --> 00:01:40,853
This is where we introduce special
nodes called switches that can

19
00:01:40,853 --> 00:01:43,632
direct traffic to the destination.

20
00:01:43,632 --> 00:01:49,066
Then in this case,
rather than this kind of shared mediums,

21
00:01:49,066 --> 00:01:53,862
the cables, each of the nodes
get this kind of point to

22
00:01:53,862 --> 00:01:57,717
point links to connect them to the switch.

23
00:02:00,803 --> 00:02:07,633
So addressing, we've already
covered addressing in Ethernet.

24
00:02:07,633 --> 00:02:14,879
So as a reminder, recall that each
network interface card has a MAC address.

25
00:02:14,879 --> 00:02:20,242
And so that's what's shown with the AA,

26
00:02:20,242 --> 00:02:23,315
BB:BB, and so forth.

27
00:02:23,315 --> 00:02:30,850
And we also showed that each frame,
which is how Ethernet provides structure,

28
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:36,369
contains both the source and
destination MAC addresses.

29
00:02:36,369 --> 00:02:38,534
So shown here is an example where A,

30
00:02:38,534 --> 00:02:43,311
the node on the left wants to talk to
the node on the right, which we'll call C.

31
00:02:43,311 --> 00:02:48,836
And their MAC addresses match their
node names just for illustration.

32
00:02:48,836 --> 00:02:54,103
And so here the frame would
say the destination is C,

33
00:02:54,103 --> 00:02:57,933
all C's, the source is all A's, and

34
00:02:57,933 --> 00:03:01,902
that is what node A sends to the switch.

35
00:03:01,902 --> 00:03:08,132
And then the switch's job is
to direct it over to node C.

36
00:03:08,132 --> 00:03:15,961
So how it does this is that each switch
has what's known as a MAC address table.

37
00:03:15,961 --> 00:03:23,177
So this is a table in the switch that maps
destination addresses to output ports.

38
00:03:23,177 --> 00:03:26,421
So when the switch receives a frame,

39
00:03:26,421 --> 00:03:31,662
it looks up the destination and
then it will send the frame.

40
00:03:31,662 --> 00:03:36,750
So it sees the destination is all C's,
it looks in this table and

41
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:41,196
says that's output port 2 and
then sends it on its way.

42
00:03:41,196 --> 00:03:45,277
And this is how the communication happens.

43
00:03:45,277 --> 00:03:49,952
So now a key question that
you might be asking is, well,

44
00:03:49,952 --> 00:03:56,634
how does this table actually get filled so
that the table starts empty, right?

45
00:03:56,634 --> 00:04:04,131
So now A, same scenario,
sends the pack or same to the frame to C.

46
00:04:04,131 --> 00:04:11,409
Well, that same lookup that we just
showed will result in no table entry.

47
00:04:11,409 --> 00:04:17,508
So this is where something called MAC
learning or MAC table learning comes in.

48
00:04:17,508 --> 00:04:22,820
So showing this scenario,
when A on the left-hand

49
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:28,006
side sends it, and
then the switch has no entry for

50
00:04:28,006 --> 00:04:32,096
destination MAC address C, all C's.

51
00:04:32,096 --> 00:04:37,576
What it does is it floods
the frame to all ports,

52
00:04:37,576 --> 00:04:42,653
but the actual destination will ignore it.

53
00:04:42,653 --> 00:04:48,841
So the middle node, node B, will receive
it, but it's just going to ignore it.

54
00:04:48,841 --> 00:04:50,763
All right, so
how did that help us with learning?

55
00:04:50,763 --> 00:04:53,212
Well, the first part is, well,

56
00:04:53,212 --> 00:04:57,853
it at least helped us get the traffic
to where it's meant to go.

57
00:04:57,853 --> 00:05:02,886
But what did we actually
learn as part of this part of

58
00:05:02,886 --> 00:05:08,858
the transmission where when
A sent something to the switch,

59
00:05:08,858 --> 00:05:12,838
so the switch actually saw, well,

60
00:05:12,838 --> 00:05:16,841
out port 0 is wherever MAC address A is.

61
00:05:16,841 --> 00:05:21,471
So I can put that into
the MAC address table or

62
00:05:21,471 --> 00:05:25,737
the MAC table, all A's maps to port 0.

63
00:05:25,737 --> 00:05:30,174
Likewise, when node C actually
replies to that message,

64
00:05:30,174 --> 00:05:32,994
we're going to learn that case again.

65
00:05:32,994 --> 00:05:36,264
We're going to learn that all C's,

66
00:05:36,264 --> 00:05:41,124
the MAC address for
all C's is out port 2, right?

67
00:05:41,124 --> 00:05:48,940
So this is how we can go about
starting to fill the MAC tables.

68
00:05:50,847 --> 00:05:55,948
Now, we're not limited to a single switch
to connect all of the nodes you want,

69
00:05:55,948 --> 00:05:59,545
so the switches are going to
be limited in number of ports.

70
00:05:59,545 --> 00:06:03,929
So smaller switches might have 4 ports and

71
00:06:03,929 --> 00:06:08,313
larger switches might have 48 ports, so

72
00:06:08,313 --> 00:06:15,035
physical places where you can
connect a wire into or a cable into.

73
00:06:15,035 --> 00:06:16,669
And so because of this,

74
00:06:16,669 --> 00:06:20,934
you typically would need to
build some topology of switches.

75
00:06:20,934 --> 00:06:26,082
So just shown here is an example
of a tree of switches that

76
00:06:26,082 --> 00:06:31,023
were built and
then each of them has its own MAC table.

77
00:06:31,023 --> 00:06:37,843
So here maybe we've done all
completely learned the entire network,

78
00:06:37,843 --> 00:06:43,736
and in this case, it's going to say A,
B, and C are out nodes or

79
00:06:43,736 --> 00:06:48,026
out port 0 and
then D is out port 1, right?

80
00:06:48,026 --> 00:06:53,177
So whenever it receives a frame for
D, it would send it out 1.

81
00:06:53,177 --> 00:07:00,333
And anytime it receives a frame for
A, B, or C, it sends it out port 0.

82
00:07:00,333 --> 00:07:05,283
A challenge with once you
start creating topologies is

83
00:07:05,283 --> 00:07:08,413
something called loops, right?

84
00:07:08,413 --> 00:07:13,345
So recall that there
are broadcast MAC addresses.

85
00:07:13,345 --> 00:07:19,246
This is when the destination
was set at all F's.

86
00:07:19,246 --> 00:07:24,187
And recall, we just saw that
we used flooding for learning.

87
00:07:24,187 --> 00:07:26,685
So let's look at what happens now.

88
00:07:26,685 --> 00:07:31,799
Let's say the MAC address
tables are all empty.

89
00:07:31,799 --> 00:07:36,501
This node on the left wanted
to send a frame to some

90
00:07:36,501 --> 00:07:39,721
destination that's not known.

91
00:07:39,721 --> 00:07:42,515
So this switch here on the left-hand side,

92
00:07:42,515 --> 00:07:46,741
the bottom left switch says I looked
it up, I don't see an entry for

93
00:07:46,741 --> 00:07:50,311
the destination,
I'm going to flood it to my neighbors.

94
00:07:50,311 --> 00:07:57,746
So it's going to send it to this top node,
top switch, and the rightmost switch.

95
00:07:57,746 --> 00:08:02,880
Then when each of those switches get it,
they look up in their MAC address

96
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:08,364
tables and see I don't have an entry
either, and then they're going to flood.

97
00:08:08,364 --> 00:08:14,774
So here this top node flooded
down to the bottom right node.

98
00:08:14,774 --> 00:08:19,146
This bottom right node said
I also don't have the entry,

99
00:08:19,146 --> 00:08:22,346
I'm flooding it to the bottom left node.

100
00:08:22,346 --> 00:08:26,382
And then what happens at that point?

101
00:08:26,382 --> 00:08:31,043
Well, this bottom left node, if we follow
the same logic, we'll look up in its

102
00:08:31,043 --> 00:08:35,794
table, see that it doesn't have an entry
for it, and we'll again flood it out.

103
00:08:35,794 --> 00:08:40,607
So it'll send it back to the top node,
it will send it back to the right node,

104
00:08:40,607 --> 00:08:43,392
but that's obviously not the behavior, or

105
00:08:43,392 --> 00:08:48,079
maybe it won't send out to the right,
but it'll send it down to the left.

106
00:08:48,079 --> 00:08:52,838
But that's not the behavior we
want because now we're going to

107
00:08:52,838 --> 00:08:56,754
amplify this traffic that
we have in our network,

108
00:08:56,754 --> 00:09:00,691
just trying to learn where
the destinations are.

109
00:09:00,691 --> 00:09:04,527
So we need to deal with loops.

110
00:09:04,527 --> 00:09:10,964
All right, so a solution to loops is we
could keep track of the messages seen so

111
00:09:10,964 --> 00:09:16,707
that bottom left switch could say,
hey, I saw this message before,

112
00:09:16,707 --> 00:09:20,590
or this frame before,
I'm going to drop it now.

113
00:09:20,590 --> 00:09:23,971
That requires it to keep a lot of state,

114
00:09:23,971 --> 00:09:28,384
which we obviously don't or
we might not want to do.

115
00:09:28,384 --> 00:09:31,517
We could limit the number of
hops a given message takes.

116
00:09:31,517 --> 00:09:37,272
So let it loop, but maybe after five,
we assume that the maximum

117
00:09:37,272 --> 00:09:42,295
number of hops that a given
frame would take is say five.

118
00:09:42,295 --> 00:09:46,251
And then after five,
we'll just drop the packet.

119
00:09:46,251 --> 00:09:52,796
And so we don't need to store state,
but it gets stored in the frame itself.

120
00:09:52,796 --> 00:09:57,118
We could also turn off loop
links that create the loop.

121
00:09:57,118 --> 00:10:05,051
So there's protocols out there called
spanning tree protocols that make it so

122
00:10:05,051 --> 00:10:09,377
that you can communicate everywhere, but

123
00:10:09,377 --> 00:10:16,967
transforms a network into one that has
no loops in it by shutting off links.

124
00:10:16,967 --> 00:10:21,625
So this keeps you from using
the full kind of bandwidth

125
00:10:21,625 --> 00:10:26,089
available in the network,
but it prevents loops.

126
00:10:26,089 --> 00:10:30,747
And then a last technique that
you might take is we can actually

127
00:10:30,747 --> 00:10:34,960
architect the network such
that looping won't occur,

128
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:39,082
whether this is create
the topology in a specific way or

129
00:10:39,082 --> 00:10:42,868
make it so
that we avoid broadcasts and flooding.

130
00:10:42,868 --> 00:10:49,457
So maybe it's that we pre-populate
the MAC address tables,

131
00:10:49,457 --> 00:10:54,282
so that you don't have
to perform learning.

132
00:10:54,282 --> 00:10:59,443
So in Ethernet, spanning tree
protocol is pretty popular as

133
00:10:59,443 --> 00:11:04,617
is architected the network
such that looping won't occur.

134
00:11:04,617 --> 00:11:11,447
And that largely occurs in data
centers that you might see.

135
00:11:11,447 --> 00:11:15,458
All right, so
that was Ethernet and the wired.

136
00:11:15,458 --> 00:11:20,028
In wireless there's a lot of similarities,
but

137
00:11:20,028 --> 00:11:26,279
really in this case, the special
nodes are called access points.

138
00:11:26,279 --> 00:11:31,549
So access points are commonly
connected to a wired network,

139
00:11:31,549 --> 00:11:36,921
and then so they use that wired
network of Ethernet switches,

140
00:11:36,921 --> 00:11:42,320
for example, to be able to
communicate beyond its own range.

141
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:48,138
And then for all the nodes, all
the traffic goes via the access points.

142
00:11:48,138 --> 00:11:53,049
So even though A and B,
for example, are in range,

143
00:11:53,049 --> 00:11:59,347
if A wants to talk to B,
A will always go via the access point AP1.

144
00:11:59,347 --> 00:12:04,494
Now the key challenge here that
we're not going to get into is

145
00:12:04,494 --> 00:12:09,551
the nodes themselves associating
with an access point.

146
00:12:09,551 --> 00:12:14,646
So you can kind of read more
on your own separately,

147
00:12:14,646 --> 00:12:19,622
but wanted to kind of this
is the parallel device or

148
00:12:19,622 --> 00:12:23,904
the similar device to an Ethernet switch.

149
00:12:23,904 --> 00:12:27,782
So with that,
we've wrap up the link layer.

150
00:12:27,782 --> 00:12:33,387
So next,
we're going to get into the network layer.

151
00:12:33,387 --> 00:12:38,779
But first,
there's interesting assignment that you're

152
00:12:38,779 --> 00:12:43,843
going to be able to kind of play
with the network traffic and

153
00:12:43,843 --> 00:12:50,116
be able to manipulate it with a tool or
a Python library called Scapy.

154
00:12:50,116 --> 00:12:50,616
Thanks.